8 #edb/ref 17592186054628 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050782 #edb/ref 1031 "The Arithmetic of Functions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050782 #edb/ref 1033 140 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050782 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "66aee53c-aecf-5378-b318-bdc0930f5e43" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050979 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049548 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050979 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049737 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050979 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050727 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050979 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050825 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050979 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186059740 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050979 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186059741 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050979 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186059742 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050979 #edb/ref 1031 "Scalar Multiplication of Matrices" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050979 #edb/ref 1033 146 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050979 #edb/ref 1034 0.5021296343794196 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050979 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "76be574f-7aed-5702-a936-6383d4a34609" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124297 #edb/ref 1069 "e-4787" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124297 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $L$ be the set of all polynomials with real coefficients whose degree is less than or equal to $2.$  Which of the following statements are true? \n\nI. For any $p(x),q(x) \\in L,$ we have $p(x)+q(x) = q(x)+p(x).$\n\nII. For any $p(x),q(x),r(x) \\in L,$ we have $[p(x)+q(x)]+r(x) = p(x)+[q(x)+r(x)].$\n\nIII. For each $p(x) \\in L,$ we have $-p(x) \\in L$ such that $p(x)+(-p(x))=0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124297 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1a855d24-cb78-5398-89e1-3bfda6907f36" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124297 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's examine our statements one at a time.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any polynomials $p(x), q(x) \\in L,$ we obtain that \n\n$$\np(x) + q(x)= q(x) +p(x)\n$$\n\nsince polynomial addition is commutative.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any polynomials $p(x),q(x),r(x) \\in L,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n[p(x) + q(x)] + r(x) = p(x) + [q(x) + r(x)]\n$$\n\nsince polynomial addition is associative.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any polynomial $p(x) \\in L,$ there is a corresponding negative polynomial $-p(x)$ such that\n\n$$\np(x) + (-p(x))= p(x) - p(x) = 0.\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\deg(-p(x)) = \\deg(p(x)) \\leq 2,\n$$\n\nhence $-p(x) \\in L.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124400 #edb/ref 1069 "e-8229" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124400 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $P$ be the set of all $2\\times 2$ real matrices $A= (a_{ij})$ such that $a_{11} + a_{22} > 0.$ Which of the following properties are true?\n\nI. For any $A \\in P,$ we have $1 \\cdot A = A.$\n\nII. For any $A \\in P$ and $\\alpha,\\beta \\in \\mathbb{R},$ we have $\\alpha(\\beta A) = (\\alpha\\beta) A.$\n\nIII. For any $A \\in P$ and $\\alpha,\\beta \\in \\mathbb{R},$ we have $(\\alpha+\\beta) A= \\alpha A + \\beta A.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124400 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "57bf5fb6-02e9-56a1-9641-342123f45406" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124400 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's verify the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any $2\\times 2$ matrix $A = (a_{ij}) \\in P,$ the entries are just real numbers. Thus, we have\n\n$$\n1 \\cdot a_{ij} = a_{ij},\n$$\n\nand hence,\n\n$$\n1\\cdot A=A.\n$$\n\n- Statements II is true. Indeed, for any $2\\times 2$ matrix $A = (a_{ij}) \\in P,$ the entries are just real numbers. Thus, for any $\\alpha, \\beta \\in \\mathbb{R},$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\alpha(\\beta a_{ij}) = (\\alpha\\beta) a_{ij},\n$$\n\nand hence,\n\n$$\n\\alpha(\\beta A) = (\\alpha\\beta) A.\n$$\n\n- Statements III is true. Indeed, for any $2\\times 2$ matrix $A = (a_{ij}) \\in P,$ the entries are just real numbers. Thus, for any $\\alpha, \\beta \\in \\mathbb{R},$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n(\\alpha+\\beta) a_{ij} = \\alpha a_{ij} + \\beta a_{ij},\n$$\n\nand hence,\n\n$$\n(\\alpha+\\beta) A= \\alpha A + \\beta A.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124432 #edb/ref 1069 "e-9960" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124432 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $M$ be the set of all $2\\times2$ real matrices whose entries are non-negative. Then, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $I_2 \\in M$\n\nII. For any $A \\in M$ and $\\alpha \\in \\mathbb{R}$, we have $\\alpha \\cdot A \\in M.$\n\nIII. The set is closed under scalar multiplication." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124432 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ff4fdeaf-bdb3-54d7-b5e9-2f3ad88b55a1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124432 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's verify the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, ![](images/05/05984749a7ebbc4315a775cc1dd628030c440891e9e902779f1a472b3be349cf.svg) because all its entries are non-negative.\n\n- Statement II is false. There exists a $2\\times2$ real matrix in $M$ that has a scalar multiple not in $M.$ \n\nFor example, let ![](images/bb/bb4cbc16280f0fe489b5842999c145f913ab9776a9abb12d228bf189ba0eea4c.svg) and $\\alpha=-1\\in\\mathbb{R}.$ Notice that $A$ belongs to $M$ since statement I is true. However,\n![](images/d6/d67cdce6dcf1167f849806639ff28e1de394dd9d81c0c9a9cb0a17e33a0da1c6.svg)\nSince the entries on the main diagonal are negative, we conclude that $-1 \\cdot A \\notin M.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, the set is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124484 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186124765 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124484 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186125219 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124484 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186243917 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124484 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186243920 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124484 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186243926 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124484 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186243929 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124484 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186243932 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124484 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124484 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124484 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281509 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124484 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360469 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124484 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360471 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124484 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360473 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124484 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186369901 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124484 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186426551 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124484 #edb/ref 1039 "Verifying Properties Involving Vector Addition" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124484 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186124297 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124484 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "7eb09138-45df-5e6c-87af-772980ff7546" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124589 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186124767 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124589 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186125242 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124589 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186243955 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124589 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186243956 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124589 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186243957 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124589 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281525 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124589 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281526 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124589 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281527 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124589 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360475 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124589 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360476 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124589 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360478 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124589 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360481 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124589 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186369915 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124589 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186371264 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124589 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186426550 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124589 #edb/ref 1039 "Verifying Properties Involving Scalar Multiplication and the Distributive Laws" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124589 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186124400 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124589 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "099e7caf-085a-5fd5-ad3d-1ebf189527b7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124628 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049304 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124628 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049755 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124628 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050782 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124628 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050979 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124628 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186125240 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124628 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186125241 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124628 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186278339 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124628 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186278340 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124628 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186278341 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124628 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186278342 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124628 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281458 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124628 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323707 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124628 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329743 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124628 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186440653 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124628 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186440654 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124628 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186440655 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124628 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying Sets Closed Under Addition and Scalar Multiplication" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124628 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186124432 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124628 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "cb02b67c-90d9-5baf-b00e-590e9271d89e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124706 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125341 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124706 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125342 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124706 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125521 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124706 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125522 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124706 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125523 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124706 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125590 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124706 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125600 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124706 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125601 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124706 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124706 #edb/ref 1045 "Introduction to Abstract Vector Spaces" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124706 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186125523 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124706 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049737 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124706 #edb/ref 1050 600.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124706 #edb/ref 1050 600.0 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186124706 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "27341ce7-bad3-5506-8e90-5fea806b76fd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124767 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243587 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124767 #edb/ref 1069 "q-106806" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124767 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124767 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $S$ be the set of all $2\\times 2$ real matrices with determinant $1.$ Which of the following statements are true?  \n\nI. For any $A\\in S,$ we have $1\\cdot A=A.$\n\nII. For any $A\\in S$ and $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we have $(\\alpha +\\beta )A=\\alpha A+\\beta A.$\n\nIII. There is a matrix $A\\in S$ such that $\\frac{1}{2}(2A)\\ne A.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124767 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a98e5d99-323b-544b-bce7-36fa3d3ba9e1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124767 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's examine each of our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any $2\\times 2$ matrix $A=({a}_{ij})\\in S,$ the entries are just real numbers. Thus, we have\n\n$$\n1\\cdot {a}_{ij}={a}_{ij},\n$$\n\nand hence,\n\n$$\n1\\cdot A=A.\n$$\n\n- Statements II is true. Indeed, for any $2\\times 2$ matrix $A=({a}_{ij})\\in S,$ the entries are just real numbers. Thus, for any $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n(\\alpha +\\beta ){a}_{ij}=\\alpha {a}_{ij}+\\beta {a}_{ij},\n$$\n\nand hence,\n\n$$\n(\\alpha +\\beta )A=\\alpha A+\\beta A.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. For a matrix in $A\\in S,$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}(2A)=(\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2)A=1\\cdot A=A\n$$\n\nsince the scalar multiplication of matrices is associative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125219 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344725 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125219 #edb/ref 1069 "q-74700" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125219 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125219 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $U$ be the set of polynomials of the form $a{x}^{3},$ where $a\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The zero-polynomial belongs to $U.$\n\nII. For any $p(x),q(x)\\in U,$ we have $p(x)+q(x)=q(x)+p(x).$\n\nIII. For any $p(x),q(x),r(x)\\in U,$ we have $[p(x)-q(x)]-r(x)=p(x)-[q(x)-r(x)].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125219 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "73f62269-52b9-5341-87d2-165ee03b0821" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125219 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the zero-polynomial can be represented as \n\n$$\n0=0\\cdot {x}^{3}.\n$$\n\n \nHence, $0\\in U.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any polynomials $p(x),q(x)\\in U,$ we obtain that \n\n$$\np(x)+q(x)=q(x)+p(x)\n$$\n\nsince polynomial addition is commutative.\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, consider polynomials $p(x)={x}^{3},$ $q(x)=2{x}^{3},$ and $r(x)=3{x}^{3}$ from $U.$ Then: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[p(x)-q(x)]-r(x) & =[{x}^{3}-2{x}^{3}]-3{x}^{3} \\\\  & =-{x}^{3}-3{x}^{3} \\\\  & =-4{x}^{3} \\\\ p(x)-[q(x)-r(x)] & ={x}^{3}-[2{x}^{3}-3{x}^{3}] \\\\  & ={x}^{3}-[-{x}^{3}] \\\\  & =2{x}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $[p(x)-q(x)]-r(x)\\ne p(x)-[q(x)-r(x)].$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125241 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360340 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125241 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94694" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125241 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125241 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $U$ be the set of polynomials of the form $a{x}^{3},$ where $a\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $h(x)\\in U,$ where $h(x)=3x.$\n\nII. For any $p(x)\\in U$ and $k\\in R,$ we have $k\\cdot p(x)\\in U.$\n\nIII. The set is closed under scalar multiplication." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125241 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1d4d76d9-0c38-5edc-a8b9-c85d1bae32b3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125241 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's examine each of our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The set $U$ contains only polynomials of the form $a{x}^{3},$ where $a$ is a real coefficient. However, $h(x)=3x$ is *not* a polynomial of this form. So, $h(x)\\notin U.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, suppose $p(x)=a{x}^{3}\\in U,$ where $a$ is a real coefficient, and $k\\in R.$ Then,\n\n$$\nk\\cdot p(x)=k(a{x}^{3})=(ka){x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nSince $(ka)$ is a real coefficient, we conclude that $(ka){x}^{3}\\in U,$ i.e., $k\\cdot p(x)\\in U.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the set is closed scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125341 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125342 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125341 #edb/ref 1061 17452 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125341 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f746dfa7-8c4b-5575-86b0-34ab4d9d09e6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125341 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125759 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125342 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125522 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125342 #edb/ref 1061 17454 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125342 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "22604921-be1a-5e8f-bdd7-8ac9e59c63a0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125342 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124484 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125521 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125601 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125521 #edb/ref 1061 30785 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125521 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7b0d0289-9feb-5b9b-9cdf-8e8bea357afd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125521 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124589 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125522 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125521 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125522 #edb/ref 1061 30789 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125522 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "605b2c91-28e4-589e-9ea6-93545635ab1c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125522 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125832 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125523 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125600 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125523 #edb/ref 1061 30790 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125523 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "befa8020-1248-59aa-b092-5fd7f5b45c2e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125523 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125833 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125590 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125341 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125590 #edb/ref 1061 37506 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125590 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "78f1d4af-7528-5705-8b7b-224b627efd47" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125590 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124628 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125600 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125590 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125600 #edb/ref 1061 37838 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125600 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1402186a-b64d-5406-9767-99815ff7ecc1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125600 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125860 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125601 #edb/ref 1061 37839 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125601 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "be30c408-bfbc-549b-b14c-3f8724059c1b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125601 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125861 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125759 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's now compare some properties of addition in $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and $\\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R}).$ \nIt turns out that the addition of two matrices in $\\mathrm{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$ is *very* similar to the addition of two vectors in $\\mathbb{R}^n.$ See the table below.\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\" width=\"700\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30\"><b></b></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"200\"><b>$\\mathbb{R}^2$</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"200\"><b>$\\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$</b></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"3\"><strong>Addition is Commutative:</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">A1</td>\n<td align=\"center\">For any vectors $\\mathbf{u}, \\mathbf{v} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2,$ we have $\\color{blue}\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{v}+\\mathbf{u}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">For any matrices $A, B \\in \\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R}),$ we have $\\color{blue}A+B = B+A$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"3\"><strong>Addition is Associative:</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">A2</td>\n<td align=\"center\">For any vectors $\\mathbf{u}, \\mathbf{v}, \\mathbf{w} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2,$ we have $\\color{blue}(\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v})+\\mathbf{w} = \\mathbf{u}+(\\mathbf{v}+\\mathbf{w})$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">For any matrices $A, B, C \\in \\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R}),$ we have $\\color{blue}(A+B) + C = A + (B+C)$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"3\"><strong>Existence of a Unique Zero Element:</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">A3</td>\n<td align=\"center\">For the zero-vector $\\mathbf{0}$ and any $\\mathbf{u} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2,$ we have $\\color{blue} \\mathbf{u} + \\mathbf{0} = \\mathbf{u}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">For the zero-matrix $O$ and any $A \\in \\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R}),$ we have $\\color{blue} A + O = A$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"3\"><strong>Existence of a Unique Opposite Element:</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr><td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">A4</td>\n<td align=\"center\">For any vector $\\mathbf{u} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$ we can find the corresponding negative vector $-\\mathbf{u} \\in \\mathbb{R}$ such that $\\color{blue} \\mathbf{u} + (-\\mathbf{u}) = \\mathbf{0}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">For any matrix $A \\in \\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$ we can find the corresponding negative matrix $-A \\in \\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$ such that $\\color{blue} A + (-A) = O$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\nAnd it's not just matrices that have additive properties similar to those of $\\mathbb R^n.$ Many mathematical structures do. Let's see some more examples." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125759 #edb/ref 1065 3184 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125759 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "e21e1a72-75b8-5ae3-9b7d-96f08d2ffa86" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125759 #edb/ref 1067 "Addition of Vectors" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125832 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's also compare some properties of scalar multiplication in $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and $\\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R}).$ It turns out that the multiplication of a matrix in $\\mathrm{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$ by a scalar is *very* similar to the multiplication of a vector in $\\mathbb{R}^n$ by a real number. See the table below.\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\" width=\"700\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30\"><b></b></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"200\"><b>$\\mathbb{R}^2$</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"200\"><b>$\\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$</b></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"3\"><strong>Invariance Under Multiplication by $1$:</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr><td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">A5</td>\n<td align=\"center\">For any vector $\\mathbf{u} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$, we have $\\color{blue} 1 \\cdot \\mathbf{u} = \\mathbf{u}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">For any matrix $A \\in \\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$, we have $\\color{blue} 1 \\cdot A = A$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"3\"><strong>Scalar Multiplication is Associative:</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr><td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">A6</td>\n<td align=\"center\">For any numbers $\\alpha, \\beta$ and any vector $\\mathbf{u} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$, we have $\\color{blue} \\alpha (\\beta \\mathbf{u}) = (\\alpha\\beta) \\mathbf{u}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">For any numbers $\\alpha, \\beta$ and any matrix $A \\in \\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$, we have $\\color{blue} \\alpha (\\beta A) = (\\alpha\\beta) A$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"3\"><strong>Distributive Laws:</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr><td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">A7</td>\n<td align=\"center\">For any numbers $\\alpha, \\beta$ and any vector $\\mathbf{u} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$, we have $\\color{blue} (\\alpha + \\beta)\\mathbf{u} = \\alpha \\mathbf{u} + \\beta \\mathbf{u}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">For any numbers $\\alpha, \\beta$ and any matrix $A \\in \\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$, we have $\\color{blue} (\\alpha + \\beta)A = \\alpha A + \\beta A$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr><td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">A8</td>\n<td align=\"center\">For any number $\\alpha$ and any vectors $\\mathbf{u}, \\mathbf{v} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$, we have $\\color{blue} \\alpha (\\mathbf{u} + \\mathbf{v}) = \\alpha \\mathbf{u} + \\alpha \\mathbf{v}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">For any number $\\alpha$ and any matrices $A, B \\in \\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$, we have $\\color{blue} \\alpha (A + B) = \\alpha A + \\alpha B$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125832 #edb/ref 1065 4934 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125832 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "05cdf1b2-a823-5410-8df1-42fa4da19240" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125832 #edb/ref 1067 "Scalar Multiplication and the Distributive Laws" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125833 #edb/ref 1064 "The structure $\\mathbb{R}^n$ is called a **vector space**. However, many other structures possess properties similar to $\\mathbb{R}^n.$ These other structures can be called vector spaces too. \n\nIn this lesson, we'll learn about some important properties used to construct vector spaces. We begin by discussing what it means for a set to be **closed** under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nAs an example, consider the set $\\mathrm{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$ of all $2 \\times 2$ matrices with real entries:\n\n![](images/a5/a53ec7e9416ae5eee4a1408df358302567a5aa98a393d6ddcb19e861645d2317.svg)\n\nFor this set of matrices, we can define two operations:\n\n- the addition of two matrices, and\n\n- the multiplication of a matrix by a real number (called a scalar).\n\nNow, notice the following:\n\n- $\\mathrm{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$ is **closed under addition**. This means that the sum of *any* two matrices from $\\mathrm{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$ is also a matrix in $\\mathrm{M}_2(\\mathbb{R}).$\n\n- $\\mathrm{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$ is **closed under scalar multiplication**. This means that the product of *any* scalar and *any* matrix from $\\mathrm{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$ is also a matrix in $\\mathrm{M}_2(\\mathbb{R}).$\n\nIdentifying that a set is closed under addition and scalar multiplication is an essential first step in determining whether a set can form a vector space." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125833 #edb/ref 1065 4935 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125833 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "18653f19-b4cf-5f0f-ab39-b1ae33ecdc6b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125833 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125860 #edb/ref 1064 "It's important to realize that not all sets are closed under addition and scalar multiplication. Sets not closed under addition and scalar multiplication *cannot* be vector spaces. \n\nFor instance, let $S$ be the set of all polynomials with real coefficients whose degree is greater than or equal to $5.$  \n\nWe note that $S$ is not closed under addition. To see why this is the case, let's define the polynomials $p(x),q(x)\\in S$ as \n\n$$\np(x) = x^5, \\qquad q(x) = 1-x^5.\n$$\n\nAdding the two polynomials using regular polynomial addition, we get\n\n$$\np(x) + q(x) = x^5 + (1-x^5) = 1\\notin S\n$$\n\nThe sum $p(x) + q(x)$ is not in $S$ because it is a polynomial of degree zero, which is less than $5.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that our set $S$ is *not* closed under addition." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125860 #edb/ref 1065 6230 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125860 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "b014039e-61b6-58d7-8f35-a72e6153c126" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125860 #edb/ref 1067 "Sets That Are Not Closed" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125861 #edb/ref 1064 "In this lesson, we discussed several properties exhibited by vectors in $\\mathbb R^n$ and other sets.\nLet's consolidate these properties into a single list.\n\n| **Label** | **Name** | **Description** |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| A1 | **Addition is Commutative** | For any vectors $\\mathbf{u}, \\mathbf{v} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2,$ we have $\\color{blue}\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{v}+\\mathbf{u}$ |\n| A2 | **Addition is Associative** | For any vectors $\\mathbf{u}, \\mathbf{v}, \\mathbf{w} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2,$ we have $\\color{blue}(\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v})+\\mathbf{w} = \\mathbf{u}+(\\mathbf{v}+\\mathbf{w})$ |\n| A3 | **Existence of a Unique Zero Element** | For the zero-vector $\\mathbf{0}$ and any $\\mathbf{u} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2,$ we have $\\color{blue} \\mathbf{u} + \\mathbf{0} = \\mathbf{u}$ |\n| A4 | **Existence of a Unique Opposite Element** | For any vector $\\mathbf{u} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$ we can find the corresponding negative vector $-\\mathbf{u} \\in \\mathbb{R}$ such that $\\color{blue} \\mathbf{u} + (-\\mathbf{u}) = \\mathbf{0}$ |\n| A5 | **Invariance Under Multiplication by $1$** | For any vector $\\mathbf{u} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$, we have $\\color{blue} 1 \\cdot \\mathbf{u} = \\mathbf{u}$ |\n| A6 | **Scalar Multiplication is Associative** | For any numbers $\\alpha, \\beta$ and any vector $\\mathbf{u} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$, we have $\\color{blue} \\alpha (\\beta \\mathbf{u}) = (\\alpha\\beta) \\mathbf{u}$ |\n| A7 | **Distributive Laws:** | For any numbers $\\alpha, \\beta$ and any vector $\\mathbf{u} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$, we have $\\color{blue} (\\alpha + \\beta)\\mathbf{u} = \\alpha \\mathbf{u} + \\beta \\mathbf{u}$ |\n| A8 | **Distributive Laws:** | For any number $\\alpha$ and any vectors $\\mathbf{u}, \\mathbf{v} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$, we have $\\color{blue} \\alpha (\\mathbf{u} + \\mathbf{v}) = \\alpha \\mathbf{u} + \\alpha \\mathbf{v}$ |\n\nWe'll meet these properties again in a future lesson, where we'll formally define what we mean by a vector space." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125861 #edb/ref 1065 6231 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125861 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "72e9ea87-7b8e-5d00-821a-31813b203a2b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125861 #edb/ref 1067 "A Summary of the Addition and Scalar Multiplication Properties of Euclidian Space" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241278 #edb/ref 1080 "I, II, and III" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241278 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241278 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0aea2174-dfeb-5a96-8044-c2efb4e92930" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241283 #edb/ref 1080 "III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241283 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241283 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0bd5bbe3-da17-5476-84db-f70827deccc2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241452 #edb/ref 1080 "III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241452 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241452 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1f09035c-a73f-5bc0-b1e9-62489ebb423b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241902 #edb/ref 1080 "I, II, and III" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241902 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241902 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "50e801e2-503a-559b-936f-f3458cb4c980" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186242150 #edb/ref 1080 "II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186242150 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186242150 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "6c0376d8-916a-5c20-b820-b920ea81d4ae" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186242166 #edb/ref 1080 "II and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186242166 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186242166 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "6eb163a6-5ef1-5d54-b94c-23b0b50d862c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186242352 #edb/ref 1080 "II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186242352 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186242352 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "86070dcd-5d3e-5df7-96bd-50a77e905437" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186242725 #edb/ref 1080 "I, II, and III" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186242725 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186242725 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "b16b4442-ffc4-5d04-a805-24e8546e2030" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186242896 #edb/ref 1080 "I and II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186242896 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186242896 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c36f8aea-29cb-5b79-a559-1287d80828c0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186242932 #edb/ref 1080 "I and II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186242932 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186242932 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c66a9c3d-3226-5038-8929-f96ac925e96f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243016 #edb/ref 1080 "II and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243016 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243016 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d059ad96-5ba2-538b-be61-12470c585a77" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243206 #edb/ref 1080 "I and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243206 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243206 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "e35537bf-65b9-5ffa-a98b-f238d2931250" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243376 #edb/ref 1080 "III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243376 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243376 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f976c12a-1c69-5418-8d74-80ce87edd353" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243392 #edb/ref 1080 "I and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243392 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243392 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "fb0726ff-286a-5ad2-a5e5-8b4b1997a03c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243403 #edb/ref 1080 "I and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243403 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243403 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "fc5f3220-69a4-5a66-9699-c599ac5f66f1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243587 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186241283 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243587 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186242166 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243587 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186242725 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243587 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186242896 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243587 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186243392 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243587 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243587 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243587 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186242896 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243587 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "4d03d1e2-dfbd-5d9a-b466-6eebce1e667d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243741 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186241902 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243741 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186242150 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243741 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186242932 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243741 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186243206 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243741 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186243376 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243741 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243741 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243741 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186242932 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243741 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "a62f5fb8-4988-5219-9bbb-89bef4000d32" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243744 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186241278 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243744 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186241452 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243744 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186242352 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243744 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186243016 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243744 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186243403 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243744 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243744 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243744 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186243016 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243744 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "a8d3769e-1942-5ecd-9ab4-4be4b985cc68" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243920 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243741 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243920 #edb/ref 1069 "q-106688" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243920 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243920 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $S$ be the set of all $2\\times 2$ matrices with real entries and determinant $1.$ Which of the following statements are true?  \n\nI. The identity matrix $[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ belongs to $S.$\n\nII. For any $A\\in S,$ we can find the corresponding negative matrix $-A\\in S$ such that $A+(-A)=O.$\n\nIII. There are matrices $A,B,C\\in S,$ such that $(A+B)+C\\ne A+(B+C).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243920 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e2555255-85b1-529f-85fb-94b6f9684401" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243920 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|=1.$ Hence, $[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\in S.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any matrix $A\\in S,$ there is a corresponding negative $2\\times 2$ matrix $-A$ such that\n\n$$\nA+(-A)=A-A=O.\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n|-A|=|-1\\cdot A|=(-1)^{2}\\cdot |A|=1\\cdot 1=1,\n$$\n\nhence $-A\\in S.$\n\n- Statement III is false. For any matrices $A,B,C\\in S,$ we have\n\n$$\n(A+B)+C=A+(B+C).\n$$\n\nsince matrix addition is associative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243956 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243744 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243956 #edb/ref 1069 "q-106810" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243956 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243956 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $J$ be the set of all polynomials with real coefficients and whose degree is at most $2.$  Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\frac{1}{x}\\in J$\n\nII. For any $p(x)\\in J,$ we have $1\\cdot p(x)\\in J$\n\nIII. For any $p(x),q(x)\\in J$ and $\\alpha \\in R,$ we have $\\alpha (p(x)-q(x))=\\alpha p(x)-\\alpha q(x)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243956 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "253cf041-a7dc-53ad-a439-778e84d95095" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243956 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's examine each of our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The set $J$ contains only polynomials whose degree is at most $2.$ However, $\\frac{1}{x}$ is *not* a polynomial. So, $\\frac{1}{x}\\notin J.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any $p(x)\\in J$ and for any fixed $x\\in R,$ the result of $p(x)$ is just a real number. Thus,\n\n$$\n1\\cdot p(x)=p(x).\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any $p(x),q(x)\\in J,$ and for any fixed $x\\in R,$ the results of $p(x)$ and $q(x)$ are just real numbers. Thus, for any $\\alpha \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\alpha (p(x)-q(x))=\\alpha p(x)-\\alpha q(x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275521 #edb/ref 1080 "III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275521 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275521 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0505ac77-e2b4-55fc-93d3-b5a995a10068" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186276116 #edb/ref 1080 "II and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186276116 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186276116 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "47edec66-5b6e-5e74-b129-043bf025b643" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186276428 #edb/ref 1080 "I only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186276428 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186276428 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "69ecde40-105d-5ae7-b3a6-93c807eea4f9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186276686 #edb/ref 1080 "II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186276686 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186276686 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "86ab6a2f-0ffb-5554-a882-c016f39dc3e6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186276919 #edb/ref 1080 "I and II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186276919 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186276919 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "9ff3d228-d3cd-5f92-9c74-48d0f6503805" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186277744 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186275521 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186277744 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186276116 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186277744 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186276428 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186277744 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186276686 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186277744 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186276919 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186277744 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186277744 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186277744 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186276428 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186277744 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "09df3955-21fb-588f-972a-b4988221d963" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278339 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277744 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278339 #edb/ref 1069 "q-160861" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278339 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278339 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $K$ be the set of all functions $f:R\\to R$ such that $f(0)=-1.$  Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $p(x)\\in K,$ where $p(x)=(x-1)^{3}.$\n\nII. For any $f(x),g(x)\\in K$, we have $f(x)+g(x)\\in K.$\n\nIII. The set is closed under addition." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278339 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d50910b7-4560-52e1-8087-133e3875c50e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278339 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's verify the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $p(x)=(x-1)^{3}\\in K$ because \n\n$$\np(0)=(0-1)^{3}=(-1)^{3}=-1.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. There exist functions in $K$ such that their sum is *not* in $K.$ For example, let $f(x)=-1$ and $g(x)=(x-1)^{3}.$ Notice that both these functions belong to $K$ since $f(0)=g(0)=-1.$ However,\n\n$$\nf(0)+g(0)=-1+(0-1)^{3}=-2\\ne -1.\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x)+g(x)\\notin K.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, the set is *not* closed under addition.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278744 #edb/ref 1080 "III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278744 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278744 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "10c955f2-1f95-51a2-98f0-3eb576e26d1b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278889 #edb/ref 1080 "I only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278889 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278889 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2048c5b4-3996-5c47-9a17-90d26